Third-party material quoted in this repository
==============================================

Neither LICENSE nor LICENSE-docs can grant rights in material that is not ours
to license. This file records what is quoted here, and on what basis.


Canadian federal legislation
----------------------------

The repository quotes and paraphrases the Citizenship Act, R.S.C. 1985, c. C-29,
as amended — most extensively in src/lib/c3/rules.ts, src/lib/c3/bars.ts,
src/lib/c3/deeming.ts and src/lib/c3/fgl.ts — along with the Canadian
Citizenship Act, S.C. 1946, c. 15 and the amending Acts S.C. 2008, c. 14
(Bill C-37), S.C. 2014, c. 22 (Bill C-24) and S.C. 2025, c. 5 (Bill C-3).

Federal statutes are subject to Crown copyright. They may be reproduced without
permission under the Reproduction of Federal Law Order, SOR/97-318, which
permits reproduction of enactments and of decisions of federally constituted
courts and tribunals

    provided due diligence is exercised in ensuring the accuracy of the
    materials reproduced and the reproduction is not represented as an official
    version.

Both conditions are taken seriously here. The rules were encoded against the
consolidated text of section 3 as last amended 15 December 2025, read directly
rather than from memory. Nothing in this repository or on the site is an official
version of any enactment; the /sources page links to the authoritative text at
laws-lois.justice.gc.ca, and every rule trace cites the provision it applies so
that a reader can check it against the real thing.


ATIP release 1A-2025-14201
--------------------------

Short passages of this 157-page release are quoted in code comments and in the
advisory text — the internal guidance on paragraph 3(1)(q) at pp. 134-136, the
two excluded cohorts at p. 155, and the processing-pause bulletin of
11 December 2025 at p. 157. The ORG ID table at pp. 63-65 is transcribed in
src/lib/c3/orgIds.ts.

Records released under the Access to Information Act remain subject to Crown
copyright. Disclosure does not place them in the public domain. The quotations
here are short excerpts, attributed, used for research and criticism, and relied
on as fair dealing under section 29 of the Copyright Act. They are reproduced
because a tool that encodes a rule drawn from a partly-redacted government
document owes its users the passage it relied on, including the fact that the
governing guidance on one point is withheld under section 23.

The release itself is not committed to this repository. The /sources page links
to a published copy.

The access-to-information request that produced this release was filed by Marin
Immigration Law, not by this project. See the /sources page.


Community material
------------------

The GCMS case notes discussed in the r/CanadianbyDescent thread linked from
/sources are quoted only in summary — a paragraph cascade, with no personal
details. They are treated as evidence of how one file was actually decided, and
they are what settles one documented decision in src/lib/c3/precedence.ts. The
thread is linked rather than reproduced.

No personal or family data from any source appears in this repository. The test
fixtures in src/lib/c3/fixtures.ts are synthetic: they reproduce the shape of a
chain and the paragraphs it should produce, never a real person's dates. See the
personal-data section of .gitignore.
